WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
A yeast cell has no ruler.

2
00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:07.220
So how does it know when it is big enough to divide, and why would being too big

3
00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:08.220
hurt?

4
00:00:11.017 --> 00:00:16.577
One thing a cell can sense is a concentration: the amount of something, divided by

5
00:00:16.577 --> 00:00:20.297
the room it has. The gauge on the right reads it.

6
00:00:21.497 --> 00:00:22.617
Watch ten red dots.

7
00:00:23.597 --> 00:00:27.737
The number stays the same, the room grows, and the concentration falls.

8
00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:33.727
A leading model, still debated, says budding yeast uses this as a ruler.

9
00:00:35.107 --> 00:00:39.567
Each newborn daughter, whatever its size, gets about the same dose of a brake

10
00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:41.847
protein called Whi5.

11
00:00:43.087 --> 00:00:46.467
It holds back Start, the point where the cell commits to dividing.

12
00:00:47.617 --> 00:00:51.697
As the cell grows, the brake spreads through more room and the gauge sinks.

13
00:00:52.957 --> 00:00:55.937
At the green line the brake gives way, and the cell commits.

14
00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:01.530
Now two newborns, one small and one large, each with the same red dose.

15
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.550
Which must grow more before it commits?

16
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:06.850
Pause and decide.

17
00:01:12.383 --> 00:01:18.783
The small one. Its dots start crowded, so its gauge starts higher, and it must grow

18
00:01:18.783 --> 00:01:19.783
more to dilute them.

19
00:01:21.003 --> 00:01:23.283
That works only because both got the same dose.

20
00:01:24.663 --> 00:01:27.383
In this simple model, both commit at the same size.

21
00:01:28.763 --> 00:01:31.823
Real cells are noisier, and close only part of the gap.

22
00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:38.667
That is the one idea: amount over volume.

23
00:01:39.847 --> 00:01:43.807
As a cell grows, it dilutes anything whose amount does not keep pace.

24
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.850
For most other contents, dilution is a danger.

25
00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.410
RNA and protein, the orange dots, do the cell's work.

26
00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:57.270
So most growing cells make them roughly in step with their volume.

27
00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.830
Their gauge stays flat.

28
00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:05.890
Their total amount grows with the cell, but their concentration does not, so it

29
00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:06.890
cannot be the ruler.

30
00:02:07.933 --> 00:02:09.573
Two things can fall behind.

31
00:02:10.853 --> 00:02:14.773
First, the genome, the cell's DNA, drawn as the purple block.

32
00:02:16.253 --> 00:02:21.193
Yeast held so they grow without dividing kept one copy while they swelled many times

33
00:02:21.193 --> 00:02:22.193
over.

34
00:02:22.583 --> 00:02:27.843
Their synthesis fell behind, so their cytoplasm, the material outside the nucleus,

35
00:02:27.943 --> 00:02:30.743
was diluted: the orange dots thinned out.

36
00:02:32.303 --> 00:02:33.803
RNA is copied from the DNA.

37
00:02:35.223 --> 00:02:38.823
At such sizes, one genome copy appears to become a limit.

38
00:02:42.867 --> 00:02:44.467
The second is the surface.

39
00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:49.327
Everything the cell takes in crosses its membrane, the blue rim.

40
00:02:50.547 --> 00:02:53.007
But it uses what it takes in throughout its volume.

41
00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.340
Take a sphere, the simplest shape, and double its radius.

42
00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.640
The rim's area rises four times, the volume eight.

43
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.640
So each bit of the inside has half as much membrane feeding it.

44
00:03:09.983 --> 00:03:11.923
So one gauge does four jobs.

45
00:03:13.143 --> 00:03:15.323
A diluted brake may tell yeast its size.

46
00:03:16.743 --> 00:03:20.803
RNA and protein keep pace, so their concentrations stay steady.

47
00:03:22.043 --> 00:03:27.223
A single genome can fall behind, and so can a sphere's surface: two costs of being

48
00:03:27.223 --> 00:03:27.563
big.
